  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields, including `Carrier`, `Cancelled` and `timestamp`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-c5e46a18 Flights\" has two panels: \"Flights over time\" (a line chart of the count by `timestamp`) and the carrier panel (a bar chart of the count by `Carrier`, top 5 by default).\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-c5e46a18 Cancelled flights\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every minute. It fires when the number of documents with `Cancelled: true` in the last 5 minutes is greater than 10. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards. It has no actions attached, so it won't notify anyone when it fires.\n4. **Panel edit and read-back:** I renamed the carrier panel to \"Flights by carrier\"; the update response shows the new title. Reading the rule back returned `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
